and
```java
/** Models the local client's own view: the host it was given, on the port it was given or the default. */
private Result resolution(List<String> command) {
    String host = command.getLast();
    String alias = resolvedHosts.getOrDefault(host, host);
    int port = 22;
    int portOption = command.indexOf("-p");
    if (portOption >= 0) port = Integer.parseInt(command.get(portOption + 1));
    return new Result(0, "hostname " + alias + "\nport " + port + "\n", "");
}
```
Careful ordering: `command.contains("-G")` — the resolution command's first element is "ssh"; `SshCommandLine.ssh(target)` also starts with "ssh" and contains... e.g. `["ssh", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", "-o", "UserKnownHostsFile=...", "-o", "StrictHostKeyChecking=yes", "-N", "-o", "ExitOnForwardFailure=yes", "-L", ...]` — no "-G" ✓. And the installer's ssh script commands are `["ssh", ...opts, destination, "sh", "-c", script]` — hmm! The script could contain "-G"?? The scripts contain things like `uname -s`, `tar -xzf`, `--health-check`... Hmm, "-G" as a standalone element? The elements are the option args and the script string (which is quoted/joined). `command.contains("-G")` checks for an exact element "-G" — the script element is a long single string, so it'd never be exactly "-G" ✓. But safer: check that it's the *second-to-last*-ish structure... Let me instead check `command.getFirst().equals("ssh") && command.get(command.size() - 2).equals("-G")` — the resolution argv ends with [..., "-G", host] ✓. That's precise and robust. 

Hmm, but then `failCommands` (stderr scripted) shouldn't affect the resolution ✓ (order matters: check the -G case before failCommands). And in `aHostKeyChangeOnARecordedHostFails`, `failCommands=true` + recorded key → no resolution failure issue ✓.

Also careful: the FakeRunner's `connected()` checks for "uname -s" ✓ unaffected.

Also `everyTerminalOperationWritesOneAuditEvent...`: `recordOnPort("deploy-alias", 2222)` + request with explicit port 2222 → the resolution argv has `-p 2222` → the fake reports port 2222 → effective target (deploy-alias, 2222) → recorded entry `[deploy-alias]:2222` matches ✓ → proceeds to the tunnel ✓.

Hmm wait, but the recordOnPort helper: `SshKnownHosts.inDataDirectory(directory).record(new SshTarget(host, ..., OptionalInt.of(port), ...), ...)` — must be updated to SshHostKeyTarget with `matchesKnownHostsField`... the recorded line is `[deploy-alias]:2222 ssh-ed25519 <blob>` and the target is (deploy-alias, 2222) → matches ✓.

