```java
    /**
     * §11.5: a {@code ~/.ssh/config} alias is a legitimate target. {@code ssh-keyscan} reads no configuration
     * and OpenSSH validates the resolved {@code HostName}, so the scan has to run against the endpoint and the
     * key has to be pinned under the name the connection will look up — while the alias stays the identity the
     * user submitted, and its {@code User}/{@code Port} stay the configuration's.
     */
    @Test
    void aNamedConfigAliasIsScannedAndPinnedUnderTheNameOpenSshValidates() throws Exception {
        var runner = new FakeRunner();
        runner.resolvedHosts.put("deploy-alias", "10.0.0.9");
        runner.scanOutput = "10.0.0.9 ssh-ed25519 " + BLOB;
        try (var orchestration = orchestration(runner, Duration.ofSeconds(5), Duration.ofSeconds(5))) {
            RemoteOrchestration.Snapshot submitted = orchestration.submit(connect("deploy-alias", "c-1"));
            RemoteOrchestration.Snapshot awaiting =
                    await(orchestration, submitted.operationId(), RemoteOrchestration.State.AWAITING_HOST_KEY_CONFIRMATION);

            assertEquals("deploy-alias", awaiting.hostKey().orElseThrow().host(),
                    "the identity the user submitted is the one shown back to them");
            assertEquals(List.of("ssh", "-G", "deploy-alias"),
                    runner.commands.stream().filter(command -> command.get(command.size() - 2).equals("-G"))
                            .findFirst().orElseThrow());
            assertEquals(List.of("ssh-keyscan", "-p", "22", "-T", "10", "10.0.0.9"),
                    runner.commands.stream().filter(command -> command.getFirst().contains("ssh-keyscan"))
                            .findFirst().orElseThrow(),
                    "the scan runs against the resolved endpoint: ssh-keyscan cannot read an alias");

            orchestration.confirm(submitted.operationId(), awaiting.hostKey().orElseThrow().fingerprint());

            assertEquals(RemoteOrchestration.State.RUNNING,
                    await(orchestration, submitted.operationId(), RemoteOrchestration.State.RUNNING).state());
            assertTrue(runner.awaitTunnel());
            assertEquals("10.0.0.9 ssh-ed25519 " + BLOB, Files.readString(knownHostsFile()).strip(),
                    "the pinned name is the one the connection validates, not the alias");
            String tunnel = runner.background.getFirst();
            assertFalse(tunnel.contains("-p "), "an omitted port is the configuration's to supply");
            assertFalse(tunnel.contains("@"), "an omitted user is the configuration's to supply");
        }
    }
```
Hmm: `SCANNED_KEY` is "deploy-alias ssh-ed25519 <base64>". I need the blob separately. Let me define in the test: `String blob = SCANNED_KEY.substring(SCANNED_KEY.lastIndexOf(0x20) + 1);`

Also careful with the `runner.commands` filter for the scan: `command.getFirst().contains("ssh-keyscan")` ✓ (the scanner's clients include the full path form; the FakeRunner's default `scanOutput` handling matches by `contains("ssh-keyscan")`).

Hmm, the scan client here: `SshHostKeyScanner` default constructor uses `localClients()` → "ssh-keyscan" first (PATH lookup happens at exec time, not in the test). The scanner instance in RemoteOrchestration is `new SshHostKeyScanner()` → clients = ["ssh-keyscan", ...absolute paths found on this machine]. So `command.getFirst()` may be "ssh-keyscan" or an absolute path — `contains("ssh-keyscan")` handles both ✓. But the assertion `List.of("ssh-keyscan", "-p", "22", "-T", "10", "10.0.0.9")` would fail if the first client returned no keys and the scanner fell back... it returns keys on the first client ✓ (`scanOutput` non-empty). And the first client is "ssh-keyscan" ✓ (LinkedHashSet order).

Hmm, but careful: the *terminal* `-T 10` value is `CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 10` ✓.

`tunnel.contains("-p ")` — the tunnel command is `["ssh", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", "-o", "UserKnownHostsFile=...", "-o", "StrictHostKeyChecking=yes", "-N", "-o", "ExitOnForwardFailure=yes", "-L", "24191:127.0.0.1:24191", "-L", "24192:127.0.0.1:24192", "deploy-alias"]` joined by spaces. Hmm! `contains("-p ")`: is there any chance of a false positive? The known-hosts path... `UserKnownHostsFile=C:\...\ssh-known-hosts` — no "-p " ✓. Hmm, `-o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes` no. OK. But hmm, safer: assert on the *list* rather than the joined string:
