```
`background` is `List<String>` (joined). Let me instead assert `assertFalse(tunnel.contains(" -p "))` and check the destination is exactly "deploy-alias" (no user@): `assertTrue(tunnel.endsWith(" deploy-alias"))` ✓ and `assertFalse(tunnel.contains("@"))` ✓ — hmm, "@" appears in... `UserKnownHostsFile=/export/user\...` no @ ✓. OK.

Hmm, careful: " -p " with spaces both sides. The joined tunnel starts with "ssh -o ..." — `-p ` could match if an option were exactly "-p". Fine, use " -p ".

Wait, but the destination being "deploy-alias" and not "10.0.0.9" is important: the connection uses the *user's* host spelling so that config (User/Port/IdentityFile) still applies ✓. Good assertion.

